<p>U.S. lawmakers are moving quickly to reassert Congress’s role in decisions about war, as President Donald Trump’s administration defends expanded U.S. participation in the U.S.-Israel war on Iran. On March 3, Trump’s top national security advisers went to Capitol Hill to brief members and press the case for an operation they described as fast-moving—one that could still involve additional steps, including the possibility of U.S. troops, depending on how the conflict develops. Congress is now preparing its first votes aimed at limiting the President’s ability to continue or widen military action against Iran without explicit authorization. In the Senate, a War Powers Resolution led by Sen. Tim Kaine would require an end to U.S. involvement in hostilities against Iran unless Congress approves continuing. In the House, lawmakers are weighing competing proposals from different factions over how tightly to constrain the operation. The debate is also sharpening questions about the administration’s objectives and legal authority. Lawmakers are pressing for clearer explanations of what success looks like and what could come after the initial strikes.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/0Dt9_OX3g6-ULdagKsqhD8u1c5OgeIsLfWtDbVYhHxp1hKTH3NFsjpCaTt7X9tB8o0tBX8YodMsECc_xCABHG5mMVgnUgIiRz7-kMwXMDxQm' alt='A view of the U.S. Capitol building in Washington, D.C., in a Reuters file photo.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Post-briefing demands: After the closed-door briefings, Democrats and some Republicans said they still want more detail—especially on the operation’s goals and what might come next.</li><li>Kaine’s urgency argument: Kaine said U.S. troops’ lives are at risk and that Congress should return to Washington to vote, rather than let the conflict continue without authorization.</li><li>Competing House paths: The Khanna–Massie measure would direct withdrawal from hostilities without authorization, while Gottheimer’s centrist-backed effort stresses consultation and compliance with existing law without demanding an immediate stop.</li><li>Vote timing: The Senate was expected to vote March 4, with the House expected to consider its approach afterward.</li></ul><blockquote>The Constitution says we’re not supposed to be at war without a vote of Congress - Senator Tim Kaine</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Trump administration national security team: Advisers told lawmakers the Iran operation is evolving rapidly and argued for congressional backing, while not ruling out additional steps such as deploying U.S. troops. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/trumps-national-security-team-comes-convince-congress-back-iran-war'>Al-Monitor (Reuters)</a>)</li><li>Sen. Tim Kaine: Kaine is invoking the 1973 War Powers Resolution and argues Congress should vote before the U.S. remains in or expands hostilities against Iran. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978174/us-congress-considers-moves-to-curtail-trumps-war-powers'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Rep. Ro Khanna and Rep. Thomas Massie: The lawmakers are advancing a bipartisan House resolution to compel withdrawal from hostilities absent congressional authorization. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978174/us-congress-considers-moves-to-curtail-trumps-war-powers'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Rep. Josh Gottheimer and allies: Gottheimer and other centrists are pressing for compliance with the War Powers Act and consultation, without calling for an immediate halt to operations. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978174/us-congress-considers-moves-to-curtail-trumps-war-powers'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Democratic senators (as described by CBS): Democrats questioned the urgency of the Iran strikes and backed war-powers measures intended to stop the conflict and prevent future wars without congressional approval. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/video/democrats-raise-doubts-about-urgency-of-trumps-iran-strikes/'>CBS News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/trumps-national-security-team-comes-convince-congress-back-iran-war'>Trump&#x27;s national security team comes to convince Congress to back Iran war</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978174/us-congress-considers-moves-to-curtail-trumps-war-powers'>US Congress considers moves to curtail Trump’s war powers</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/video/democrats-raise-doubts-about-urgency-of-trumps-iran-strikes/'>Democrats raise doubts about urgency of Trump&#x27;s Iran strikes</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/us-politics/article-congress-to-take-first-votes-on-iran-war-as-debate-rages-about-us/'>Congress to take first votes on Iran war as debate rages about U.S. goals</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/trumps-national-security-team-comes-to-convince-congress-to-back-iran-war'>Trump&#x27;s national security team pushes war on Iran in Congress</a> - straitstimes.com</li></ul>